---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { MethodologyAccordion } from "../../components/AnalysisPageSections";
---

<Layout
  role="visitor"
  breadcrumbs={[
    { label: "Home", href: "/" },
    { label: "Methods", href: "/methods/evaluation-framework" },
    { label: "Limitations" },
  ]}
>
  <div class="mx-auto px-4 sm:px-8 py-12 max-w-4xl">
    <div class="mb-10">
      <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-dark mb-3">Limitations</h1>
      <p class="text-gray-600 text-lg">
        Important caveats to keep in mind when interpreting results from the
        impact analysis and MCDA tools on the SUM platform.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Impact analysis limitations -->
    <div class="mb-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-dark mb-4 border-b border-gray-200 pb-2">
        Impact Analysis Limitations
      </h2>
    </div>

    <MethodologyAccordion
      title="Interpreting regression coefficients"
      subtitle="What the model can and cannot tell you"
      content=""
      defaultOpen={true}
    >
      <div class="space-y-4 text-sm text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p>Results should be interpreted with the following in mind:</p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-3 ml-4">
          <li>
            If a measure was implemented uniformly across all Living Labs, its
            individual effect may be harder to isolate, and results should be
            read with caution.
          </li>
          <li>
            The <strong>mean squared error (MSE)</strong> of the model provides
            an indication of estimation accuracy. A lower MSE means the model
            fits the observed data more closely, but even a low MSE does not
            imply causal certainty.
          </li>
          <li>
            The regression model estimates <strong>association</strong>, not
            causation. Unobserved factors (e.g. external economic conditions,
            parallel policy changes) may influence KPI outcomes independently of
            the measures recorded.
          </li>
          <li>
            Results are most reliable when a diverse set of Living Labs
            implements different combinations of measures, enabling the model to
            differentiate individual contributions.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </MethodologyAccordion>

    <MethodologyAccordion
      title="Data constraints"
      subtitle="How data volume affects result reliability"
      content=""
    >
      <div class="space-y-4 text-sm text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-3 ml-4">
          <li>
            The number of Living Labs (observations) is small relative to the
            number of measures (predictors). Ridge regularization mitigates
            overfitting, but estimates remain less precise than in
            large-scale observational studies.
          </li>
          <li>
            KPIs with few data points (e.g. local KPIs measured by only 2–3
            labs) produce wider confidence intervals. Multivariate analysis
            groups KPIs by type to partially address this.
          </li>
          <li>
            Missing before/after data pairs reduce the effective sample. Labs
            with only one measurement time point are excluded from regression
            but appear in trend charts.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </MethodologyAccordion>

    <!-- MCDA limitations -->
    <div class="mb-8 mt-12">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-dark mb-4 border-b border-gray-200 pb-2">
        MCDA Limitations
      </h2>
    </div>

    <MethodologyAccordion
      title="Interpreting PROMETHEE rankings"
      subtitle="What rankings do and do not express"
      content=""
      defaultOpen={true}
    >
      <div class="space-y-4 text-sm text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-3 ml-4">
          <li>
            Rankings reflect the specific set of criteria and weights used in
            each analysis. Changing perspective weights can change rankings
            significantly — this is expected behavior, not a flaw.
          </li>
          <li>
            A high PROMETHEE rank does not guarantee a measure will be effective
            in a specific city. Rankings reflect relative performance across the
            alternatives in the analysis set, not absolute effectiveness.
          </li>
          <li>
            Alternatives not included in the analysis set do not appear in the
            ranking, even if they might outperform those included.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </MethodologyAccordion>

    <MethodologyAccordion
      title="GAIA plane interpretation caveats"
      subtitle="When to rely more on flow-based results"
      content=""
    >
      <div class="space-y-4 text-sm text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-3 ml-4">
          <li>
            <strong>GAIA quality below 60%</strong> indicates the 2D projection
            loses significant decision information. In this case, rely on the
            PROMETHEE net flow ranking rather than visual GAIA positions.
          </li>
          <li>
            The decision stick represents the overall PROMETHEE direction, not
            an absolute recommendation. It summarises the model's view given
            the current weights.
          </li>
          <li>
            Criterion vectors of similar length dominate the GAIA plane equally.
            If one criterion has a very long vector, small changes in its weight
            may strongly alter the ranking.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </MethodologyAccordion>

    <!-- General limitations -->
    <div class="mb-8 mt-12">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-dark mb-4 border-b border-gray-200 pb-2">
        General Data Limitations
      </h2>
    </div>

    <MethodologyAccordion
      title="Platform-wide data caveats"
      subtitle="Limitations that apply across all tools"
      content=""
      defaultOpen={true}
    >
      <div class="space-y-4 text-sm text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-3 ml-4">
          <li>
            The platform covers Living Labs within the SUM project. Results may
            not generalise to cities or contexts outside the project scope.
          </li>
          <li>
            Data is self-reported by Living Lab administrators and subject to
            the quality control process described on the{" "}
            <a
              href="/methods/data-quality"
              class="text-primary hover:underline font-medium"
            >
              data quality page
            </a>. Errors in submission affect downstream analysis.
          </li>
          <li>
            The platform reflects data submitted at a point in time. Ongoing
            measures may show incomplete before/after pairs until post-intervention
            data is submitted.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </MethodologyAccordion>
  </div>
</Layout>
